    {
      "id": "stage-note",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 560,
      "y": 110,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "The 1032 nm beam is folded down onto the combiner, 200 mm of path the other arm does not have.\nA mechanical stage carrying a fold changes the path by 2Δx for a move of Δx; this delay line adds its ΔL directly.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "delay-note",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 150,
      "y": 480,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "ΔL = 200 mm = 0.667 ns matches the fold, so both pulses reach the crystal together and the sum-frequency line is there.\nRetune it by 0.06 mm — 200 fs — and about a quarter of that peak is left; by 0.2 mm it is gone,\nwhile the two second harmonics stay exactly where they were. That is the measurement.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "legend",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 650,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "Behind the crystal: 395 nm and 516 nm, the two second harmonics, and 447.5 nm, the sum frequency of the pair.\nDoubling takes 30 % of each beam and the mixing 30 % of what is left of both, so the three peaks sit in the same range: 0.30, 0.30 and 0.42 of a beam.\nBoth lasers run at 80 MHz; on a bench they would be locked to one clock, and this model mixes only trains at the same repetition rate.\nPhase matching is not modelled: the relative strengths of the three peaks are not predicted, and the polarizations each process needs are not checked.\nDifference-frequency generation is a checkbox on the crystal, left off here: its 3.4 µm line is outside the range this bench would look at.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    }
